title: Query planner
tags: [[Query engine]]
kind: reference
audience: dev
aliases: [[Planner statistics]]
summary: A minimal planner, oriented to the store: exact per-field histograms kept as a derived key space, and a cost model that tells a scattered read from a contiguous one, decide whether a scan probes its candidates or walks its range, and in which order the scans of an AND run.

The original indexing design had an ''adaptive planner'' choosing an access path per
operand. It was taken off the roadmap in September 2026: in the resident engine each
operand had exactly one way to be answered, so there was nothing to choose. On the
key-value store an operand can again be answered several ways — a posting or a walk of
the value partition, a per-candidate check or a scan of distinct values, a fetch or an
ordered walk. So a planner "may be useful again — to be seen" (user, September 2026):
minimal and oriented to the store, one cost choice at a time, from what profiling
finds. It was built in October 2026 (`index/plan.rs`) on fixed rules; the cold
measurements of November 2026 showed one of them wrong by twenty, and statistics
replaced it (user decision, 9 November 2026).

No cost model of the whole query: each choice is a rule on the query's shape, on an
exact count the store holds, or on a histogram's estimate. Other local choices live
with what they choose for: fetch or walk for a sorted page ([[Sorting and postings]]),
lazy text checks ([[Text search in the store]]), the anchors of an `osm` path
([[The forest in the store]]).

!! What the planner decides

* ''The order of an `and`'' (`plan::and_order`, `Eval::scan_estimate`). Its operands answered from a bitmap or a posting — presence, equality, `in`, a subtree, a follow, `uuid_in` — are evaluated first, in the query's order; then the ''scans'' — `matches`, `osm direct`, `!=` and the ordered comparisons —, the narrowest first, ties in the query's order, each handed the running intersection as its candidates. A scan's estimate is, for an ordered comparison of a number or a date, its histogram's `at_most` (`Source::range_estimate`); for any other, its field's holders. The estimates are read only when two scans are to be ordered. Once the intersection is empty, nothing more is evaluated.
* ''Probe or walk'' (`plan::probes`, `KvSource::probes`). A scan given candidates checks their own rows (a ''probe'') when that costs less than walking its range. A probe costs `PROBE_KEYS` (4) keys per candidate — its uuid, its rows of the field, the miss of one that holds none —, each weighed `SCATTERED` (4) contiguous keys. The walk of a `!=` or a text predicate costs the field's holders; of an ordered comparison of a number or a date, the range's `at_most` rows, less the rows of the values in the range that have a posting, plus two keys each — a posted value is walked as its posting (`range_walk`, read from the postings' own keys). The histogram is read only once the holders leave the choice open. Each row is decided by its key, as the walk decides it, so the two cannot answer differently (`tests/index_oracle.rs`).

A scan is never put before the bitmaps, however narrow: a bitmap is read whole today,
handed candidates or not, and a scan handed them reads no more than its own walk, so
evaluating it first could only cost more (Open below).

What it bought, by key count (`tests/kv_cost.rs`):

* `size > 1000000` beside a folder read the whole field (16 109 keys on 16 000 records), now the folder (`a_range_on_distinct_values_costs_the_narrow_operand`);
* a range holding 1 % of the repository, in a folder holding a fifth: 6 440 keys, now no more than the two operands alone (`a_narrow_range_in_a_wide_folder_walks_the_range`);
* a pattern every file holds and that range: 32 327 keys pattern first, 644 range first; now 644 in either order (`of_two_scans_the_narrower_goes_first`).

!! The plan depends on the data, the answer does not

Choosing differently never answers differently: an `and` commutes, and a probe and a
walk decide each row by the same key. So the plan may depend on the data without
breaking a paginated session — the cursor is tied to the query sent
([[How queries are evaluated]]). Every page gets the same ''results''; it may get them
by another path if the data changed between pages.

!! The statistics

''Exact, maintained like an index'' — a derived key space ([[Store tables]]), written
in each write's own transaction by `KvTxn::derive_row`, rebuilt by `reindex`, compared
by `check` — rather than sampled and refreshed, which would drift and make plans
depend on when the sample was taken.

* ''A histogram per ordered field'': a count of rows per ''bucket'', a bucket being a prefix of the value key (`kvstore/derived.rs`, `value_key`, `hist_bucket`), so that buckets are contiguous ranges of the partition and a range's estimate is the sum of the buckets it covers, exact but for the one the operand falls in. The prefix width is per type:
** a number is an order-preserving `f64` (`num_key`): its first 16 bits are the sign, the exponent and 4 bits of mantissa — buckets of 1/16 of an octave, log-scaled, which suits sizes and counts; `int` and `float` share the type tag, so one histogram;
** a date is milliseconds in an `i64` (`dt_key`): the first 16 bits are 9 000 years wide, so a date bucket keeps the first 34 bits — buckets of 2^30 ms, ≈ 12 days —, the rest of the fifth byte cleared;
** a text or a tree name: ''not kept'' (Open below) — its estimate is its field's holders.
* ''Set sizes'' are known exactly already: a field's holders (`present`), a posting, a subtree's descendants, a candidate set.

Only the populated buckets are stored, in the table `hist`: a key `field · bucket` →
count of rows (a record holding a value twice counts twice). A row written or removed
changes one count: one key put more per row change of a number or a date. `mf repo
check` compares each count to what the rows derive (`check_histograms`);
`DERIVED_VERSION` 6 brought the table, so an older store is reindexed when it opens.

''The estimate'', `KvSource::range_rows(field, op, value)`: the rows the comparison
holds, as `at_least` (the buckets wholly inside) and `at_most` (those and the operand's
bucket). It reads the buckets on the comparison's side of the operand — one key past
every value —, not the rows. A field has one type ([[One value type per field name]])
and an operand of another is refused ([[Comparisons]]), so the histogram of the
operand's type is the field's. Tested against a count of the rows themselves, after
writes, edits, deletions and a reindex (`tests/kv_histogram.rs`).

!! The cost model

A cardinality is not a cost: a probe's keys are a candidate's rows, then the next one's,
far apart in the file; a walk's are a hundred to a page. The model prices a plan in
''contiguous'' and ''scattered'' key reads: a walk, its rows, contiguous; a probe, its
keys per candidate, scattered; a bitmap, a key per 65 536 ids. One weight serves warm
and cold — the plan should be good cold, where the time is.

''Calibrating it'': `scripts/dev/bench.sh --calibrate-planner` measures, cold on `L`,
the slope of a walk (`mfr_size > t` at three thresholds: milliseconds against keys) and
of a probe (`uuid_in(c) and mfr_size > -1`, less `uuid_in(c)` alone, at three `c`:
milliseconds against candidates), for candidates spread over the repository and for
the files of one folder, and prints the weight (`regression/calibrate.rs`). It prints
and records nothing: the number is the machine's.

The weight 4 was a first value, from a profile read wrongly. The first calibration (9
November 2026, the machine of the tables below, not neutral) measured 3.5 µs a walked
key and 184 µs a spread candidate — 52 walked keys, a weight of 13 —, 213 µs a
candidate of one folder — 60 walked keys, 15. A probe reads ≈ 2 keys a candidate, not
`PROBE_KEYS`' 4: each is a page of its own cold. Candidates of one folder are no
cheaper on `L` than spread ones.

!! What showed it

[[The timed regression suite]]'s `--cold` pass on `L` (100 000 files), and the same
queries cold by hand after the statistics (the same machine, three runs each):

|!Scenario |!Keys after |!Cold before |!Cold after |
|`query.size_range_in_folder` — `mfr_size > 900000` below `/dir0/dir1` |11 760 |1 270–1 390 ms |87–111 ms |
|`query.size_range` — the same range over the repository |11 786 |80–92 ms |84–113 ms |
|`query.subtree_range_text` — a subtree, `rating > 4` and a name pattern |3 044 |1 270–1 320 ms |310–378 ms |
|`query.finder` |5 045 |390–420 ms |441–661 ms |

Bounding a range by a folder made it fifteen times ''slower'': 18 983 candidates × 4 =
75 932 < 100 000 holders of `mfr_size`, so the rule then — probe when the candidates
cost less than the field's holders — probed, reading 38 815 scattered keys (27 ms warm,
1.5 s cold), where the walk reads the 11 785 rows ''inside the range''.
`subtree_range_text` was the same case on `rating`. What is left of it is its text leaf
on the forest's names, checked on the page; of the finder, the `osm` path's resolution
in the forest before the index runs — neither a plan's choice.

!! Measured and not built

Two choices were weighed against measurements, warm then cold, and left out — a page
under a large subtree, a two-term `osm` path: [[Planner choices measured and not built]].

!! Open

* Whether a text histogram is worth its keys (a text range is rarer than a numeric one), and the names of a forest likewise. Not kept for now: a string row costs no more to write than before. The date width was settled at 34 bits without a measurement of what a date range typically spans.
* Whether a bitmap handed few candidates should read only its chunks holding them (a set is a key per 65 536 dense ids, a chunk up to 8 KB) — never more keys than reading it whole, in the same key order. Then a scan of very few rows would be worth evaluating before the bitmaps. Nothing to gain at the sizes measured (100 000 records: two chunks a set); 16 chunks at a million, about 150 at ten. Not built (user, 9 November 2026).
* An `or`, an `and` or an `in` nested in an `and` hands its candidates down to its own scans, yet is ordered as a bitmap, first.
* Whether the name search of a finder can be made local: cold, an `osm` path on `L` costs ≈ 0.4–0.7 s, the rows of ≈ 1 100 nodes each a scattered read. Neither the order of a plan nor another formulation reads fewer of them ([[Planner choices measured and not built]]).
* Whether the probe weight should depend on how scattered the candidates are: on `L`, the files of one folder cost as much as candidates spread over the repository (the first calibration) — whether that holds of a forest-order reindex is not measured.

!! How the measurements were taken

* `scripts/dev/bench.sh --cold --big` (`--filter query.` to narrow); the generated `L` lives in `target/bench-data/L`.
* Keys per phase: a daemon of its own on another port, `./target/release/metafolder-daemon --port 7631 --config crates/daemon/default-config/config.toml`, the requests authorised by `Authorization: Bearer $(cat $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/metafolder/daemon.token)`, then `POST /repos/load` and `POST /repos/:repo/query/profile` with the query's body. Cold by hand: unload, `fsync` and `posix_fadvise(DONTNEED)` on `.metafolder/internal/kv/data.mdb`, load, wait for `GET /repos/:repo` to say `ready`.
* Stop that daemon by its pid, never with `pkill -f` on a pattern the stopping command itself contains.
* Tests count keys (`tests/kv_cost.rs`, `KvStore::reads()`), never a duration ([[Tests that fail under load]]).
